BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bigci-environmental-awards-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260803
SUMMARY:Deadline: BigCi Environmental Awards 2026
DESCRIPTION:BigCi's Environmental Awards are an annual prize for work that 
 engages with the environment\, open to Australian and international artist
 s working in visual art\, installation\, multimedia\, performance\, litera
 ture\, music\, photography\, filmmaking and curating. TWO WINNERS each rec
 eive four weeks of free residency at BigCi plus AUD 5\,000 in prize money.
  BigCi sits on the edge of Wollemi National Park in the Greater Blue Mount
 ains\, an hour and a half from Sydney but geologically another world\; win
 ners explore the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Wollemi\, Blue Mountains and
  Gardens of Stone National Parks on guided walks and are given research as
 sistance relevant to their projects. At the end of their residencies they 
 present the work at the BigCi Open Day. JUDGING: three independent judges\
 , Lea Kannar-Lichtenberger (Australia)\, Brian Wallace (China) and Dan Got
 tlieb (USA). APPLICATION: an online submission form (Wufoo) linked from th
 e awards page\, with a non-refundable AUD 40 entry fee paid at the time of
  submission. KEY DATES: submissions close 2 August 2026\; winners announce
 d 22 August 2026. Enquiries via the contact form at bigci.org/contact.\n\n
 Organisation: Bilpin International Ground for Creative Initiatives (BigCi)
 \nLocation: Bilpin\, New South Wales\, Australia\, on the edge of Wollemi 
 National Park in the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Greater Blue Mountains\,
  about 1.5 hours from Sydney. Open to Australian and international artists
 .\nAward: Two awards\, each of AUD 5\,000 prize money plus four weeks of f
 ree residency at BigCi\, guided walks in the surrounding national parks an
 d research assistance for the project. AUD 40 entry fee\, non-refundable a
 nd payable with the submission.\nDuration: Four-week residency at BigCi pe
 r winner\n\nMore: https://bigci.org/awards/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://bigci.org/awards/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Bilpin\, New South Wales\, Australia\, on the edge of Wollemi Nati
 onal Park in the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Greater Blue Mountains\, abo
 ut 1.5 hours from Sydney. Open to Australian and international artists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-australia-arts-projects-organisations-2026@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative Australia: Arts Projects for Organisations (Sept
 ember 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:Creative Australia's principal open-round project grant for art
 s organisations and the most directly relevant Australian scheme for mount
 ing festivals\, exhibitions and public programmes. The guidelines explicit
 ly list festivals\, productions\, exhibitions\, performances\, touring\, p
 ublishing\, recording\, collaborations\, research and community engagement
  as supported activity. Organisations may propose either a single project 
 or an entire programme of projects\, which can extend to a full artistic p
 rogramme for a given calendar year or longer\, so this can function as de 
 facto annual programme funding. The category is artform-agnostic and routi
 nely supports media arts\, digital culture\, experimental sound and art-an
 d-science work\; activity delivered online is explicitly permitted. NOTABL
 E: international organisations are eligible where the project demonstrably
  benefits practising Australian artists\, their work\, or Australian audie
 nces\, so a non-Australian festival can apply if it is programming Austral
 ian artists. The applicant must be a legally constituted entity: incorpora
 ted association\, company limited by guarantee\, or government statutory a
 uthority. Registration in the Application Management System must be comple
 ted at least one business day before the closing date. The round closes 1 
 September 2026 at 3pm AEST.\n\nOrganisation: Creative Australia\nLocation:
  Activity may take place nationally within Australia\, internationally\, o
 r online.\nAward: AUD 20\,000 to 100\,000. No matching or co-financing req
 uirement. No application fee. Organisational administrative costs should g
 enerally not exceed 10 percent of the total project budget or the applicat
 ion becomes less competitive.\nDuration: Activity must last no longer than
  two years from the proposed start date\; the project must commence after 
 notification and within one year of it.\n\nMore: https://creative.gov.au/i
 nvestments-opportunities/arts-projects-organisations?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://creative.gov.au/investments-opportunities/arts-projects-organis
 ations?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activity may take place nationally within Australia\, internationa
 lly\, or online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:create-nsw-stories-matter-literary-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260727
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260728
SUMMARY:Deadline: Create NSW: Stories Matter NSW Literary Fellowships 2026
DESCRIPTION:Stories Matter is a new NSW literary fellowship program from Cr
 eate NSW\, administered by Writing NSW\, awarding ten fellowships of AUD 5
 0\,000 each to support NSW-based writers to focus on a major new work over
  a 12-month period. The 2026 round funds four emerging\, three mid-career\
 , and three established writers working across prose fiction\, poetry\, il
 lustration\, graphic novels\, and playwriting. ELIGIBILITY: writers must b
 e based in New South Wales\; open to writers at any career stage within th
 e three tiers (not open to applicants outside NSW). No application fee. DE
 ADLINE: 27 July 2026\, 2:00pm AEST. Apply via the Create NSW grants portal
 .\n\nOrganisation: Create NSW (administered by Writing NSW)\nLocation: New
  South Wales\, Australia\; applicants must be NSW-based.\nAward: AUD 50\,0
 00 per fellowship\, 10 fellowships awarded (4 emerging\, 3 mid-career\, 3 
 established). No application fee.\nDuration: 12-month fellowship\n\nMore: 
 https://www.nsw.gov.au/grants-and-funding/stories-matter-nsw-literary-fell
 owships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nsw.gov.au/grants-and-funding/stories-matter-nsw-literary-f
 ellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New South Wales\, Australia\; applicants must be NSW-based.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:copyright-agency-non-fiction-writing-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Copyright Agency Cultural Fund: Fellowship for Non-Fictio
 n Writing 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Copyright Agency Cultural Fund's Fellowship for Non-Fiction
  Writing awards AUD 80\,000 to one mid-career or established Australian au
 thor to research and write a substantial new work of literary non-fiction.
  Eligible forms include memoir\, biography\, history\, criticism\, arts an
 d science writing\, and environmental and political writing. ELIGIBILITY: 
 mid-career or established Australian authors with a demonstrated publicati
 on record and publisher interest in the proposed work\; Australian residen
 ts only. APPLICATIONS open 20 April 2026 and close 17 August 2026\, 1:00pm
  AEST. Apply via the Cultural Fund fellowship page.\n\nOrganisation: Copyr
 ight Agency (Cultural Fund)\nLocation: Australia\; Australian authors only
 .\nAward: AUD 80\,000 (one fellowship). No application fee.\nDuration: Fel
 lowship to research and write a new work\n\nMore: https://www.copyright.co
 m.au/culturalfund/fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://www.copyright.com.au/culturalfund/fellowship/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia\; Australian authors only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:copyright-agency-author-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Copyright Agency Cultural Fund: Author Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Copyright Agency Cultural Fund's Author Fellowship awards A
 UD 80\,000 to one established Australian author to create a significant ne
 w work for publication. ELIGIBILITY: established Australian authors with c
 ritical acclaim and confirmed publisher interest in the proposed work\; Au
 stralian residents only. This is a separate fellowship from the Cultural F
 und's Fellowship for Non-Fiction Writing\, though administered on the same
  cycle. APPLICATIONS close 17 August 2026\, 1:00pm AEST. Apply via the Cul
 tural Fund fellowship page.\n\nOrganisation: Copyright Agency (Cultural Fu
 nd)\nLocation: Australia\; Australian authors only.\nAward: AUD 80\,000 (o
 ne fellowship). No application fee.\nDuration: Fellowship to create a new 
 work\n\nMore: https://www.copyright.com.au/culturalfund/fellowship/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.copyright.com.au/culturalfund/fellowship/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia\; Australian authors only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:blackbird-foundation-believers-fund-iii-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270630
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270701
SUMMARY:Deadline: Blackbird Foundation: Believers Fund III 2026
DESCRIPTION:Believers funds the people\, projects and programs creating the
  conditions for young people's creativity to flourish at scale. Creativity
  is meant in the broadest sense: artists are creative\, but so are scienti
 sts\, debaters\, community builders and dancers\, and a project can be any
 thing from a summer camp to a robotics competition to a co-working holiday
  house for artists. Blackbird looks for inventive\, dynamic experiments th
 at encourage young people to be curious\, to explore\, to be lifelong lear
 ners\, and to build community and become high-agency people\, backing proj
 ects that are 0-1 (just starting) or 1-10 (scaling or iterating) rather th
 an established groups. Examples: a neighbourhood community science lab\, a
  hacker house\, a hardware garage makerspace\, a travelling STEM roadshow\
 , or an after-school arts or coding club. ELIGIBILITY (no flexibility): in
 dividuals\, groups or organisations established in Australia and/or New Ze
 aland\; projects taking place in AU/NZ\; projects completed within 12 mont
 hs\; projects whose core focus is primarily creative outcomes\; and organi
 sations with less than AUD 1.5M in revenue. WHEN TO APPLY: Believers is op
 en year-round\, with EOIs and applications reviewed and progressed on a ro
 lling basis\; the program closes when all funds are disbursed or at the en
 d of the FY27 period (30 June 2027)\, whichever comes first. Expressions o
 f interest for Believers Fund III open on 30 June 2026.\n\nOrganisation: B
 lackbird Foundation\nLocation: Australia and/or New Zealand\; individuals\
 , groups or organisations established in AU/NZ\, with projects taking plac
 e in AU/NZ.\nAward: AUD 5\,000 and AUD 10\,000 grants. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Projects must be completed within 12 months\n\nMore: https://w
 ww.blackbird.foundation/projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://www.blackbird.foundation/projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia and/or New Zealand\; individuals\, groups or organisatio
 ns established in AU/NZ\, with projects taking place in AU/NZ.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wheeler-centre-next-chapter-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Wheeler Centre: The Next Chapter 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Next Chapter is The Wheeler Centre's flagship writing progr
 am\, designed to elevate Australian stories that are underrepresented and 
 to nurture a new wave of writers to tell them. Each fellow receives a AUD 
 $15\,000 stipend and is paired with a handpicked mentor for a twelve-month
  period to progress a major writing project. Fellows also receive a reside
 ncy at Varuna\, The National Writers' House\; an end-of-year publishing in
 tensive at The Wheeler Centre\; and meaningful opportunities to connect wi
 th peers\, agents\, publishers\, and readers. One fellowship place is dedi
 cated specifically to a poet: The Malcolm Robertson Fellowship. 2027 judge
 s: Melissa Lucashenko\, Micaela Sahhar\, Cher Tan\, and Melanie Saward. EL
 IGIBILITY: Australian writers. No specific genre restrictions stated\; the
  program focuses on stories that are not currently being told and voices t
 hat are underrepresented. TIMELINE: submissions open 2 June 2026\; submiss
 ions close 31 July 2026 (5pm)\; 2027 Next Chapter Fellows announced 10 Nov
 ember 2026. Questions: thenextchapter@wheelercentre.com. Apply at https://
 www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter.\n\nOrganisation: The Wheeler Centr
 e\nLocation: Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Na
 tional Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Whe
 eler Centre.\nAward: AUD $15\,000 stipend per fellow. No application fee.\
 nDuration: 12 months.\n\nMore: https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chap
 ter?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Nationa
 l Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Wheeler 
 Centre.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:richell-prize-emerging-writers-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers\, presented by the Emerg
 ing Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\, is an annual prize for unpu
 blished writers of adult fiction and adult narrative non-fiction. The winn
 er receives AUD $10\,000 and a 12-month mentorship with a Hachette Austral
 ia publisher\, who will work with the writer to develop their manuscript\,
  with first option to consider the finished work for publication. Long- an
 d short-listed writers are also eligible to apply for a Casa Morphosi Resi
 dency in Abruzzo\, Italy. The prize was established in memory of Hachette 
 Australia CEO Matt Richell (d. 2014). ELIGIBILITY: must currently reside i
 n Australia (Australian citizens\, permanent residents\, and long-term vis
 a holders residing in Australia\; those residing overseas are not eligible
 )\; unpublished writers only -- no previous publication by a publishing ho
 use (including small press or independent publishers in any genre)\; publi
 cation in anthologies\, literary journals\, scholarly works\, writing PhD 
 projects\, or self-publishing does not disqualify. ELIGIBLE GENRES: adult 
 fiction (including short story collections) and adult narrative non-fictio
 n (including memoir and essay collections). Poetry\, scripts\, plays\, pic
 ture books\, practical non-fiction\, and works for children or young adult
 s are not eligible. SUBMISSION FORMAT: single PDF comprising the first thr
 ee chapters (maximum 20\,000 words)\, a one-page synopsis\, and a chapter 
 breakdown of up to two A4 pages\; plus a statement (up to 750 words) on ho
 w winning the prize will further your writing career\, entered in the onli
 ne form. Manuscript must be complete or intended to be completed\; does no
 t need to be finished at time of submission. A4\, double-spaced\, 12pt Rom
 an font (e.g. Garamond or Times New Roman)\, 3cm margins\, page numbers. T
 IMELINE: prize opened 23 April 2026\; closes 13 July 2026 at 11:59pm AEST\
 ; longlist announced 11 September 2026\; shortlist 16 October 2026\; winne
 r announced 6 November 2026. Administered with support from Simpsons Solic
 itors. Apply at https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/
 .\n\nOrganisation: Emerging Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\nLoca
 tion: Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.\nAward: AUD $10\,000
  prize money (donated by Hachette Australia)\; 12-month mentorship with a 
 Hachette Australia publisher\; first option to consider finished work for 
 publication. Long- and short-listed writers also eligible to apply for a C
 asa Morphosi Residency in Abruzzo\, Italy. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 12-month mentorship.\n\nMore: https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/riche
 ll-prize-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:melbourne-queer-film-festival-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: Melbourne Queer Film Festival 2026 Submissions
DESCRIPTION:MQFF\, Australia's largest queer film festival\, accepts intern
 ational films of interest to LGBTQIA+ audiences. Deadline 3 August 2026\; 
 fees USD 30 for shorts and USD 50 for features\, with fee waivers reserved
  for Australian and First Nations entrants.\n\nOrganisation: Melbourne Que
 er Film Festival (MQFF)\nLocation: Melbourne\, Australia (festival 12-22 N
 ovember 2026). Open internationally.\nAward: Screening at Australia's larg
 est queer film festival. Fees USD 30 shorts / USD 50 features (waivers for
  Australian and First Nations entrants only).\nDuration: Festival 12-22 No
 vember 2026.\n\nMore: https://filmfreeway.com/MelbourneQueerFilmFestival?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://filmfreeway.com/MelbourneQueerFilmFestival?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Melbourne\, Australia (festival 12-22 November 2026). Open interna
 tionally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:helpmann-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T135731Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Helpmann Fellowships 2026
DESCRIPTION:Three Helpmann Fellowships of up to AUD 7\,500 support ambitiou
 s\, self-directed projects that advance the artistic and professional deve
 lopment of emerging creatives. Fellowships are open across all eligible di
 sciplines\, to individuals and to collectives\, and funding can go to larg
 er-scale creative projects\, tailored professional development\, or a comb
 ination of both. Previous fellowships have supported the creation of new w
 ork\, exhibitions and performances\, residencies and internships\, touring
  and recording\, specialised training\, and long-term professional develop
 ment\; each fellowship is shaped by the recipient rather than a fixed prog
 ramme. ELIGIBILITY IS NARROW AND GEOGRAPHIC: applicants must be current re
 sidents of South Australia and either final-year students or graduates wit
 hin five years of a supported degree at Adelaide University\, the Universi
 ty of Adelaide\, Flinders University\, or the University of South Australi
 a. Honours students and graduates (up to five years after the final year o
 f study) are eligible where honours was undertaken directly following the 
 undergraduate degree. Masters and PhD candidates and graduates are not eli
 gible unless they qualify through their undergraduate or honours study. Fi
 nal-year students may apply provided the funded activity takes place after
  studies are completed and within the activity timeframe. Applications are
  assessed on artistic merit\, alignment with career development\, feasibil
 ity\, commitment to learning\, and level of ambition. The online applicati
 on requires a CV (max two pages)\, an official academic transcript\, a bud
 get on the Helpmann template\, a timeline\, project confirmations\, letter
 s of support from an academic and an industry referee\, and four to six im
 ages or one to two audio-visual work samples. An online information sessio
 n runs 4 August 2026 at 6pm ACST\, and the Program Manager\, Toni Wills\, 
 takes online or in-person application chats (info@helpmannacademy.com.au).
  Applications opened 15 July 2026 and close 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm ACST
 \, for activity occurring 1 January to 31 December 2027. Free to apply.\n\
 nOrganisation: Helpmann Academy\nLocation: South Australia (applicant must
  be a current South Australian resident)\; funded activity may take place 
 anywhere.\nAward: Up to AUD 7\,500 per fellowship\, three fellowships avai
 lable. Supported by the James & Diana Ramsay Foundation. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Funded activity must occur between 1 January 2027 and 31 Dec
 ember 2027\n\nMore: https://helpmannacademy.com.au/helpmann-fellowships/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://helpmannacademy.com.au/helpmann-fellowships/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:South Australia (applicant must be a current South Australian resi
 dent)\; funded activity may take place anywhere.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
